xmodel/sol-xside.log-83509-+## 2. The x-side interface
xmodel/sol-xside.log-83510-+
xmodel/sol-xside.log:83511:+### 2.1 Exact normalized factor streams
xmodel/sol-xside.log-83512-+
xmodel/sol-xside.log-83513-+For a genuine polynomial pair, take the y-leading polynomials
xmodel/sol-xside.log-83514-+
xmodel/sol-xside.log-83515-+\[
xmodel/sol-xside.log-83516-+\begin{aligned}
xmodel/sol-xside.log-83517-+ \phi_f(x)&=[y^{126}](f-a)
xmodel/sol-xside.log-83518-+   =c_f x^{42}+c_{f,41}x^{41}+\cdots+c_{f,0},\\
xmodel/sol-xside.log-83519-+ \phi_g(x)&=[y^{189}]g
xmodel/sol-xside.log-83520-+   =c_g x^{63}+c_{g,62}x^{62}+\cdots+c_{g,0}.
xmodel/sol-xside.log-83521-+\end{aligned}                                               \tag{2.1}
xmodel/sol-xside.log-83522-+\]
xmodel/sol-xside.log-83523-+
xmodel/sol-xside.log:83524:+The branch-product factorization in `SHEET6-DIRECTIONB.md` gives the exact
xmodel/sol-xside.log:83525:+normalized, eta-independent factors
xmodel/sol-xside.log-83526-+
xmodel/sol-xside.log-83527-+\[
xmodel/sol-xside.log-83528-+\begin{aligned}
xmodel/sol-xside.log-83529-+ U_f(t)&=\frac{\phi_f(t^{-42})}{c_ft^{-1764}}
xmodel/sol-xside.log-83530-+       =1+\sum_{m=1}^{42}\alpha_m t^{42m},
xmodel/sol-xside.log-83531-+       &\alpha_m&=\frac{c_{f,42-m}}{c_f},\\
xmodel/sol-xside.log-83532-+ U_g(t)&=\frac{\phi_g(t^{-42})}{c_gt^{-2646}}
xmodel/sol-xside.log-83533-+       =1+\sum_{m=1}^{63}\beta_m t^{42m},
xmodel/sol-xside.log-83534-+       &\beta_m&=\frac{c_{g,63-m}}{c_g}.
xmodel/sol-xside.log-83535-+\end{aligned}                                               \tag{2.2}
xmodel/sol-xside.log-83536-+\]
xmodel/sol-xside.log-83537-+
xmodel/sol-xside.log:83538:+Thus the finite x-factor input sequences are
xmodel/sol-xside.log-83539-+
xmodel/sol-xside.log-83540-+\[
xmodel/sol-xside.log-83541-+  (\alpha_1,\ldots,\alpha_{42}),\qquad
xmodel/sol-xside.log-83542-+  (\beta_1,\ldots,\beta_{63}),                              \tag{2.3}
xmodel/sol-xside.log-83543-+\]
xmodel/sol-xside.log-83544-+
xmodel/sol-xside.log:83545:+with factor-coordinate levels \(42m\). If
